# Paths the PR reviewer does not read (SERV-87).
#
# One extended regex per line, matched against the repository-relative path of
# every changed file. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# Per-repo on purpose even though the workflow is shared (SERV-92): which
# artifacts are generated is a fact about this repository. The shared workflow
# reads this from the BASE ref of the PR under review, so widening it in a PR
# does not exempt that PR — and this file is itself in `sensitive_paths`, so a
# PR that widens it escalates.
#
# What belongs here is regenerated artifacts, not merely large files. The test
# is: if this file is wrong, is the fix to edit it? For a generated file the
# answer is no — you re-run the generator — so a review comment on its diff has
# nowhere to land.

# The four generated wire artefacts. One schema walked by one generator emits
# all of them, and `npm run gen:check` byte-compares each against a fresh run —
# `verify.sh` proves the guard bites once PER FILE, so none can drift from the
# schema without CI going red.
#
# This is the clearest case the test above admits: a review comment on
# `generated.go` has nowhere to land, because the fix for anything wrong in it
# is a change to `schema/codegen/generate.mjs` or to the schema. Both are in
# `sensitive_paths`, so excluding the rendered copies moves the budget ONTO the
# hand-written cause rather than away from the subject.
#
# Together they are ~4,500 lines that would otherwise be re-read on every PR
# that so much as adds a field.
^web/src/wire/generated\.ts$
^dart/lib/src/generated\.dart$
^server/internal/wire/generated\.go$
^schema/openapi\.yaml$

# release-please owns this and rewrites it wholesale. Added prospectively:
# CLAUDE.md names release-please, and nothing wires it yet (CANT-11 deliberately
# left it to the deploy path). It is also `.md`, so the docs-only rule already
# covers a release PR; this covers the case where it rides along with code.
^CHANGELOG\.md$

# NOT excluded, deliberately, and each for a reason:
#
#   schema/catenary.wire.v1.schema.json
#                   — the SOURCE, hand-written, and the thing three languages
#                     generate from. Named here so nobody excluding "the schema
#                     stuff" takes the contract out with the artefacts.
#   schema/codegen/generate.mjs
#                   — the generator. Not generated.
#   schema/vectors/vectors.json
#                   — the 41 golden vectors. Hand-written, and they ARE the
#                     oracle: a weakened vector is how a conformance suite goes
#                     quietly green. Exactly what the reviewer should read.
#   schema/mapping/wire-fields.json
#                   — hand-written decisions about what backs each wire field.
#                     `internal/store/wiremap_test.go` checks the types against
#                     the database; nothing checks whether a `derived` note is
#                     TRUE, and that is a reading job.
#   migrations/     — hand-written SQL applied at boot, fatal on failure. In
#                     `sensitive_paths`.
#   go.sum          — a dependency tree, but also the checksum database. A line
#                     changing without a matching go.mod change is the shape of
#                     a supply-chain problem, and worth a read.
#   spike/          — read-only evidence, not generated. It should almost never
#                     appear in a diff, and a PR that edits it is a PR rewriting
#                     the record — which is precisely what wants a human's
#                     attention, not an exemption.
#   verify.sh, deploy/
#                   — hand-written verification and deployment definitions.
#                     Both in `sensitive_paths`.
